## Why This Is Asked

Assesss real-world ability to design resilient, low-tail-latency multi-region pipelines under stress while preserving tenant fairness and clear rollback paths.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and circuit breaking in streaming pipelines
- Per-tenant QoS and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once processing guarantees across Kafka and sinks
- Observability for SLO-driven tail latency
- Chaos testing and incident rollback discipline

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bounded per-tenant backpressure
function handleEvent(event, tenant) {
  const q = queues[tenant];
  if (q.length < MAX_PER_TENANT) {
    enqueue(event, tenant);
  } else {
    drop(event); // apply backpressure upstream
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation plan with synthetic workloads?
- What metrics would you use to decide when to restore normal traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable alerting that reduces noise while preserving critical signals, across multi-region K8s clusters, using common tools, with safe rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Alert routing and deduplication (Alertmanager, labels)
- Global blackout policy with per-region windows
- Canary rollout for rule changes; staging validation
- Metrics: alert rate, MTTA, MTTR, missed-incident risk; rollback plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple in-memory alert suppression
const suppression = new Map(); // key -> timestamp until which alerts are muted
function shouldAlert(key) {
  const t = suppression.get(key);
  if (!t) return true;
  return Date.now() > t;
}
function suppress(key, durationMs) {
  suppression.set(key, Date.now() + durationMs);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for cross-cloud regions with drift?
- How would you validate that suppression does not hide critical incidents during outages?
